blanket — Explains generic out-of-body implementations such as `implements<T> I for Box<T>`.

Syntax:
  interface Named {
    name: string
  }
  
  interface Printable {
    function print(self) -> string
  }
  
  class Box<T> {
    value: T
  }
  
  implements<T extends Named> Printable for Box<T> {
    function print(self) -> string {
      return self.value.name
    }
  }

A blanket implementation is a generic `implements` rule that applies to every type matching the `for` target and bounds. The binder goes immediately after `implements`, before the interface target: `implements<T extends Bound> Interface for Wrapper<T> { ... }`. Each implementation generic parameter must appear in the `for` type, and associated type bindings go inside the block with `type Name = ...`.
